.TH ZEROMERGE 1 "November 2026" "zeromerge 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zeromerge \- combines files with missing (zeroed) blocks of data
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zeromerge
.I [options] file1 file2 output_file
.br
.B zeromerge
.I [options] \-r [directories/files...]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Combines two files with missing data (represented by blocks of zeroes) into
a new file that contains the "present" (non-zero) data from both files. Any
zeroed blocks in both files will remain zeroed in the output file.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-C ##
Set I/O chunk size index (0 = 4K, doubles up to 18 = 1GB; default 12 = 16MB).
.TP
.BI \-s " SIZE[suffix]"
Minimum file size to consider in recursive (\-r) mode; supports bytes or K/M/G/T[i]B suffixes.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-recursive
Recursively scan paths, group files by size, merge matches, and link/copy.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress all progress and completion output.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Output version information and exit.

.SH NOTES
If the input files are different sizes or there is non-zero data which is
different between the two files, the merging will abort. Output files are
not automatically deleted on failure.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B zeromerge ~/downloads/fileA.mp4 ~/downloads/fileB.mp4 fileMerged.mp4
.TP
.B zeromerge \-r ~/downloads/

